Sump-Jive
Ingredients: ROCKABILLY & ROCK 'N' ROLL + DUBSTEP + CYBERPUNK & DYSTOPIAN + Piccolo + 2000s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Born at an illegal rooftop rave in a flooded megacity, 2091.

Sump-Jive is the frantic, rusted collision of 1950s rockabilly swing and bone-rattling 140-BPM low-end pressure, drenched in the salt-crusted decay of a sunken metropolis. Acoustic upright slap-bass is retrofitted with crude piezo transducers and slammed into 2000s-era sub-bass wobble filters, while spit-flying, overblown piccolos substitute for twangy lead guitars—screaming like tea kettles under military-grade distortion pedals. It sounds like an amp thrown down a half-submerged fire escape: percussive, manic, grease-stained, and relentlessly athletic.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 140 to 144 BPM. The groove relies on a furious, swung rockabilly "train-chug" drum shuffle mapped onto a classic half-time dubstep grid, alternating between high-velocity triplet skittering on the hi-hats and a sledgehammer backbeat on the three.
- Harmonic Palette: Greasy, flattened-fifth blues scales collided with cold Phrygian dominant progressions; abrasive microtonal dissonance generated by bent piccolo intervals and down-tuned upright bass strings.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Hydro-Acoustic Tape Cavitation: An impossible studio method where reel-to-reel magnetic tape loops are submerged inside pressurized, salt-brine chambers, forcing the water pressure to physically drag the tape across the playhead to produce erratic, sea-swallowed slapback delay.
  2. Screamer-Piccolo Feedback Channelling: Piccolo flutes mic’d inside copper pipes and routed through vintage 2000s Line 6 DL4 delay pedals and Boss Metal Zone stompboxes, turning the dainty woodwind into a shriek resembling a blown marine-engine siren.
  3. Bitcrushed Double-Slap Sub: Upright slap-bass finger-clicks tracked hot and downsampled to 2000s 12-bit MP3 compression rates, triggering Native Instruments Massive-style sub-bass cavitation rumbles on every percussive string slap.
- Dynamics: Violent tidal shifts; tracks sprint along hyperactive rockabilly shuffles before abruptly dropping into cavernous, sub-aquatic vacuums where only an overdriven piccolo whispers, followed by explosive, swampy half-time sub-bass eruptions.

Scene & Origin Lore
In the winter of 2091, the megacity of Drowned Jakarta existed only above the fifty-fifth floor, linked by rusty zip-lines and makeshift pontoon barges. On the rain-lashed roof of the half-submerged Oshima Spire, scavengers, outlaw divers, and grease-punks lashed three diesel generators to an abandoned 2000s PA system to hold unpermitted midnight stomps. The subculture that emerged—calling themselves "Bilge-Greens"—wore high-cuff neoprene diving waders, oilskin creepers, and pompadours glued stiff with petroleum jelly and marine grease. They danced the "Bilge-Twist," an aerobic, heel-digging stomp designed to shake seawater off their clothes while dodging low-flying surveillance copters. Sump-Jive became their survival anthem: greaser swagger weaponized against drownings, corporate drone curfews, and the cold crush of the black ocean below.

Typical Instruments: Salvaged acoustic upright bass (rigged with contact piezos), concert C and wooden piccolos (run through 2000s guitar multi-effects), hollowbody archtop guitars strung with steel crane wire, 2000s VST software synthesizers (Native Instruments Massive, Propellerhead Malström), Ludwig snare drums fitted with rusted tambourine jingles.

For Fans Of: The Cramps, Benga, The Prodigy, Reverend Horton Heat.

